% ============================================================================
% measuring-tool-si-unit.query.adj — worked recall over the chemistry/
% metrology causal-composition library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "what SI unit does a
% thermometer's reading reduce to?" — it states no fact of its own — it only
% `import`s the grounded rule and asks binding queries. The answer is
% DERIVED, composing measuring-tools' specific tool→quantity assignment with
% si-base-units' general metrology principle for that quantity, so every
% answer carries BOTH citations in its `steps` trail.
%
% Placed at the stdlib PACKAGE ROOT (not beside
% `chemistry/measuring-tool-si-unit.adj`) — the same cross-directory-import
% sandboxing pattern `earth-layer-matter-behavior.query.adj` already
% established: the library itself lives in `chemistry/` and reaches its
% metrology sibling via a relative `../metrology/si-base-units.adj` import,
% which only resolves if the CONSUMING entry program (this file) sits at or
% above the package root, so both `chemistry/` and `metrology/` fall inside
% the CLI's sandboxed import subtree.
%
% Run (from this directory, the package root, so the relative import
% resolves):
%     ../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli measuring-tool-si-unit.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "chemistry/measuring-tool-si-unit.adj"

? measuring_tool_si_unit(thermometer, $U, $S)      % expect kelvin, "K" (direct: a thermometer measures temperature)
? measuring_tool_si_unit($T, kelvin, $S)            % expect thermometer (reverse: which tool's reading is in kelvin?)
? measuring_tool_si_unit(graduated_cylinder, $U, $S) % expect abstain -- volume is a DERIVED SI unit, not one of the 7 base units
